## 1. Objective and scope

### Objective
Determine:

1. Whether nominally identical 3D-printed polymer tensile coupons show reproducible between-coupon, between-build, between-printer, or between-test differences in tensile strength beyond expected measurement error.
2. Which measurable manufacturing, material, environmental, geometric, and testing factors explain those differences.
3. Which observations distinguish competing causal explanations, including:
   - feedstock variability or moisture;
   - thermal-history and extrusion variability;
   - interlayer bonding and porosity;
   - dimensional or surface defects;
   - build-position and printer effects;
   - storage and conditioning;
   - test-machine, alignment, gripping, or operator effects.
4. Whether identified effects generalize to independently produced coupons.

### Scope
Use one polymer, one nominal coupon geometry, and one primary printing process initially. Tensile strength is the primary outcome. Elastic modulus, yield strength, strain at break, failure location, and fracture morphology are secondary outcomes used to diagnose mechanisms. A later confirmation phase may vary polymer or printer model, but only after the initial causes are estimated.

The study will estimate effects for the explicitly studied material, process window, machines, and test conditions; it will not assume universal applicability to all additive-manufacturing systems.

---

## 2. Assumptions

- “Nominally identical” means coupons share the same design file and nominal print and test settings, not that their realized material state, geometry, or thermal history is identical.
- Tensile strength is calculated from peak force using both nominal and measured cross-sectional area; the measured-area result is primary.
- Coupon manufacture and testing follow a single predefined tensile-specimen standard appropriate to the polymer and process. Geometry, conditioning, loading rate, and exclusions are fixed before data collection.
- Printers and test frames can be calibrated or independently checked, and specimens can be uniquely traced from feedstock through fracture.
- Material lots can be subdivided so that feedstock lot is not perfectly confounded with printer, day, or build.
- Destructive testing makes repeated strength measurements on the same coupon impossible; therefore repeatability must be estimated using replicate coupons and nondestructive pre-test measurements.
- Manufacturing factors may act through mediators such as void fraction, dimensions, crystallinity, residual stress, or bead-interface quality. Total effects and mediation evidence will be reported separately.
- Coupon exclusions will be based on predefined technical criteria, not on strength values.
- The design will emphasize randomization, blocking, replication, and blinded testing rather than relying solely on observational correlations.

---

## 3. Research design

### Phase A: Measurement qualification and variance decomposition

Before investigating causes:

- Verify load-cell performance, extensometer performance, crosshead rate, temperature and humidity logging, dimensional instruments, balances, and relevant printer sensors.
- Conduct a crossed tensile-testing study using sacrificial, homogeneous reference specimens where feasible:
  - multiple operators;
  - multiple test frames or fixtures, if available;
  - repeated setup and gripping;
  - randomized test order.
- Estimate repeatability and reproducibility of force, dimensions, alignment, modulus, and calculated strength.
- Print an initial balanced set under the nominal recipe across multiple builds, positions, days, printers, and operators. Use this set to partition variability among:
  - material lot;
  - drying batch;
  - printer;
  - build;
  - build position;
  - test day/frame/operator;
  - residual coupon-to-coupon variation.

This phase establishes whether the persistent differences primarily track production or testing units and informs final sample-size calculations.

### Phase B: Controlled factor-screening experiment

Use a randomized, blocked factorial or definitive screening design. Select factor ranges that are plausible in routine production and do not intentionally create obviously invalid prints. Candidate factors include:

- feedstock moisture or drying condition;
- feedstock lot;
- nozzle temperature;
- build-chamber or ambient temperature;
- bed temperature;
- print speed or volumetric flow;
- cooling-fan setting;
- layer height;
- extrusion multiplier;
- raster orientation;
- infill or contour strategy;
- printer identity;
- build-plate position;
- storage duration and conditioning humidity;
- print-to-test interval.

Keep geometry and tensile procedure fixed. Record actual process values rather than relying only on setpoints.

Design principles:

- Use at least three independent builds for each critical condition; do not treat multiple coupons from one build as independent manufacturing replicates.
- Distribute each condition across printers, days, plate positions, material spools, and testing sessions.
- Ensure each printer and build day receives multiple experimental conditions where physically possible.
- Randomize condition assignment to builds and positions.
- Randomize tensile-test order independently of printing order.
- Blind testing personnel to manufacturing condition and prior nondestructive measurements.
- Include center-point or nominal-condition coupons in every build to detect temporal drift and nonlinear effects.
- Place replicated nominal coupons across the plate to estimate spatial gradients.
- Avoid changing several factors together unless required by the planned factorial contrast.
- Prespecify a small set of scientifically credible interactions, such as temperature × speed, moisture × temperature, orientation × layer height, and printer × position.

The number of builds and coupons will be selected using simulation-based power analysis from Phase A variance estimates. Power will target practically important changes in strength and variance components, with the build—not the coupon—as the replication unit for build-level interventions.

### Phase C: Mechanism-focused experiments

Use the screening results to select a limited number of leading mechanisms. Conduct targeted experiments that manipulate the proposed cause while holding rival causes constant.

Examples:

#### Feedstock moisture explanation
- Randomly assign material from the same lot to controlled dry, intermediate, and moisture-exposed conditions.
- Measure moisture immediately before printing.
- Use the same printers and balanced build positions.
- Test whether moisture predicts strength and whether its effect is mediated by extrusion instability, void fraction, or fracture morphology.

#### Thermal/interlayer-bonding explanation
- Manipulate nozzle and chamber temperature within safe limits while recording actual thermal histories.
- Pair coupons with matched geometry and density where possible.
- Assess whether strength changes preferentially for loading orientations sensitive to interlayer interfaces.

#### Under-extrusion or porosity explanation
- Manipulate volumetric flow or extrusion multiplier around the nominal setting.
- Quantify coupon mass, density, internal voids, bead geometry, and local cross-sectional defects.
- Test whether strength scales with effective load-bearing area and whether the effect remains after correcting area.

#### Build-position explanation
- Rotate and randomize standardized condition patterns over repeated plates.
- Compare fixed machine-coordinate effects with effects that follow material assignment or coupon orientation.

#### Testing or gripping explanation
- Print paired coupons from the same local build region.
- Randomly assign pairs across frames, operators, grip setups, and test orders.
- Use alignment verification, strain-field measurements, and failure-location data to determine whether apparent manufacturing differences move with the test setup.

### Phase D: Independent confirmation

Lock the model and proposed mechanism before confirmation.

- Produce new coupons on later dates using new builds and, where possible, a new material lot.
- Include both nominal conditions and predicted high- and low-strength conditions.
- Test samples in randomized, blinded order.
- Evaluate out-of-sample prediction and whether deliberately controlling the identified factor reduces the variance of nominal coupons.
- If generalization is a goal, repeat a reduced confirmation on an additional printer or polymer and treat this as a separate inference domain.

### Diagnostic contrasts distinguishing explanations

| Competing explanation | Supporting observations | Observations arguing against it |
|---|---|---|
| Material moisture/lot | Strength follows measured moisture or lot across printers and days; drying changes outcome; characteristic voids or extrusion fluctuations appear | Effect remains after moisture equalization; no dose-response; differences stay tied to printer or plate position |
| Thermal/interlayer bonding | Strength follows recorded thermal history; effects are stronger in interface-sensitive orientations; fracture follows layer boundaries | Strength changes only through dimensions; no interface-related fracture or orientation interaction |
| Porosity/under-extrusion | Lower density, mass, or effective area and greater void fraction precede lower strength; flow intervention reproduces effect | Equal internal structure and effective area despite persistent strength differences |
| Geometry or surface notch | Local minimum area, roughness, or defect predicts fracture origin and strength; area correction reduces differences | Failure initiates away from measured defects; effects persist after local geometry adjustment |
| Build-position/printer | Stable spatial or printer-specific effects recur after rotating randomized layouts | Differences follow material condition rather than machine coordinates and disappear after balancing |
| Test alignment/gripping | Results follow frame, operator, fixture, or eccentric strain; many grip failures; reassignment changes apparent group differences | Differences persist across randomized test setups with centered strain fields and valid gauge failures |
| Storage/conditioning | Strength follows measured conditioning state or print-to-test time under randomized storage | No controlled time or humidity effect despite adequate exposure contrast |
| Unmeasured stochastic defects | Residual variation is associated with localized defects but not stable process variables | A controlled factor reliably reproduces and removes the variation |

---

## 4. Data or evidence needed

### Provenance and process data
For every coupon:

- unique coupon, build, spool, material-lot, printer, nozzle, plate, operator, and test identifiers;
- design-file and slicing-software versions;
- all commanded print parameters;
- plate coordinates and coupon orientation;
- print order and timestamps;
- machine maintenance and calibration history;
- filament diameter along the consumed segment, where measurable;
- material drying, handling, storage, and measured moisture;
- ambient, chamber, bed, and nozzle temperatures;
- humidity;
- actual extrusion, speed, flow, pressure, power, or motor signals available from the machine;
- interruptions, alarms, and operator interventions;
- print-to-test interval and conditioning history.

### Coupon-level evidence before testing
- dimensions at multiple locations in the gauge section;
- local minimum cross-sectional area;
- mass and bulk density;
- warpage and surface roughness;
- nondestructive internal-defect measurements on all coupons if practical, or a randomized subset otherwise;
- images of surfaces and visible defects;
- material-state measurements relevant to the polymer, such as crystallinity, thermal transitions, or residual stress, on matched witness samples or a predefined subset.

### Tensile-test evidence
- raw synchronized force, displacement, strain, and time series;
- crosshead speed and actual strain rate;
- frame, load cell, grips, operator, and test-session identifiers;
- specimen alignment and grip pressure;
- environmental conditions;
- strain-field or extensometer data;
- peak force, strength, modulus, yield, strain at break, absorbed energy, and failure location;
- video or photographs of deformation and fracture;
- deviations from procedure.

### Post-fracture evidence
- fracture-surface images under blinded coding;
- classification of interlayer, intralayer, void-initiated, surface-defect, or grip-related failure;
- localized microscopy or compositional analysis for representative high-, low-, and median-strength specimens selected by a prespecified rule;
- agreement between independent fracture classifiers.

### Evidence quality
Maintain a complete chain of custody, immutable raw data, calibration records, data dictionaries, protocol deviations, and preregistered exclusion and transformation rules.

---

## 5. Measurement

### Primary outcome
Ultimate tensile strength:

\[
UTS_i=\frac{F_{\max,i}}{A_{0,i}}
\]

where \(A_{0,i}\) is the measured initial gauge-section area. Because printed cross sections may be irregular, estimate area at several gauge locations and define in advance whether the minimum or a specified average is primary. Report peak force and nominal-area strength as sensitivity outcomes.

### Secondary outcomes
- elastic modulus;
- yield strength or proof stress;
- strain at break;
- toughness;
- failure location and mode;
- within-build strength variability;
- fraction of invalid or grip failures.

### Manufacturing and material measurements
Define calibrations, resolution, uncertainty, and timing for:

- filament moisture;
- temperature at the nozzle, chamber, bed, and local coupon region;
- filament diameter and extrusion flow;
- coupon mass, dimensions, density, porosity, and roughness;
- layer and bead geometry;
- crystallinity or other relevant material state.

Use measured values as exposure variables where reliable, retaining setpoints as separate variables. Calibrate or cross-check printer-reported sensor values against independent references on a planned subset.

### Testing-system controls
- Verify load-cell accuracy across the relevant force range.
- Check frame and grip alignment before each testing block.
- Use consistent grip faces, pressure, insertion depth, preload, and loading rate.
- Measure strain with an appropriate extensometer or full-field method rather than relying solely on crosshead motion.
- Repeat dimensional measurements blindly on a random subset to estimate measurement error.
- Have two blinded assessors classify fracture mode for a subset and quantify agreement.

### Missingness and exclusions
Prespecify:

- valid gauge-section failure criteria;
- handling of grip failures, machine interruptions, sensor dropouts, and visibly damaged coupons;
- rules for missing process logs;
- criteria for repeating a test, with repeats retained and labeled rather than silently replacing results.

No observation will be excluded only because its strength is extreme. Robust analyses will assess the influence of anomalous but technically valid coupons.

---

## 6. Analysis

### Descriptive analysis
- Plot strength and secondary outcomes by build, printer, position, material lot, test session, and experimental condition.
- Map strength, geometry, density, porosity, and temperatures across build plates.
- Examine time-order plots for printer drift and test-frame drift.
- Compare fracture modes and locations across conditions.
- Report raw and adjusted distributions, not only group means.

### Variance decomposition
Fit hierarchical mixed-effects models reflecting the nesting and crossing of the design. A representative model is:

\[
y_{ijklmn} =
\beta_0 + X\beta +
u_{\text{lot}} + u_{\text{printer}} + u_{\text{build}}
+ u_{\text{position}} + u_{\text{test session}}
+ \epsilon
\]

Include random slopes where justified and spatial covariance for plate coordinates if residual maps indicate gradients. Estimate:

- variance attributable to material lot, drying batch, printer, build, plate position, test session, and residual coupon variation;
- intraclass correlations;
- repeatability and reproducibility;
- uncertainty intervals for every component.

Use coupon-level residual degrees of freedom cautiously because multiple coupons from one build are subsamples, not independent replicates.

### Factor-effect estimation
For the randomized screening experiment:

- estimate main effects and prespecified interactions;
- model nonlinear continuous-factor effects where center points support them;
- report effect sizes and uncertainty intervals in physical units;
- control multiplicity using a prespecified hierarchical strategy or false-discovery procedure for exploratory factors;
- verify model assumptions and use heteroscedastic or robust models when needed.

Distinguish effects on mean strength from effects on variability. A factor that leaves the mean unchanged but increases variance may still explain persistent coupon differences.

### Measurement-error treatment
Correct or account for uncertainty in dimensions, moisture, thermal measurements, and porosity using replicate-measurement or errors-in-variables models where material. Compare conclusions using:

- measured versus nominal area;
- raw peak force versus calculated strength;
- printer-reported versus independently verified process values.

### Causal and mechanistic analysis
Base primary causal claims on randomized factor assignments. Use process measurements and material-state variables to evaluate mechanisms:

1. Confirm that the randomized intervention changes the proposed mediator.
2. Confirm that the mediator precedes testing and predicts strength.
3. Determine whether adjustment for the mediator attenuates the intervention effect.
4. Check mechanism-specific signatures, such as orientation interactions, spatial patterns, or fracture modes.
5. Test rival predictions explicitly.

Mediation estimates will be labeled mechanistic evidence rather than definitive proof when mediator–outcome confounding remains possible.

### Model comparison and prediction
Compare prespecified explanatory models representing material, thermal, geometry/porosity, spatial, and testing hypotheses. Evaluate them using:

- out-of-build and out-of-day cross-validation;
- calibration and prediction error;
- residual variance reduction;
- stability of coefficients across printers and lots;
- ability to predict confirmation-phase high- and low-strength conditions.

Cross-validation folds will keep all coupons from the same build together to prevent information leakage.

### Sensitivity analyses
Repeat key analyses:

- excluding predefined invalid failures;
- including all technically interpretable failures;
- with robust likelihoods;
- by fracture mode;
- using peak force rather than strength;
- under alternate reasonable area definitions;
- with and without influential builds;
- accounting for missing process data;
- separating intervention variables from post-treatment mediators.

---

## 7. Decision / stopping rule

### Evidence for a contributing factor
A factor will be considered supported as a contributor only if:

1. Its randomized or otherwise well-controlled contrast has an effect whose uncertainty interval excludes a prespecified negligible-effect range, or it materially changes variance.
2. The direction and approximate magnitude are consistent across independent builds.
3. The effect replicates in the locked confirmation phase.
4. Results are not explained by tensile-machine, operator, grip, or area-calculation artifacts.
5. At least one predicted diagnostic signature of the proposed mechanism is observed.

A factor may be classified as predictive but not causal if it improves out-of-sample prediction without a randomized or credible quasi-experimental contrast.

### Evidence for the dominant explanation
An explanation will be designated dominant if controlling its factor:

- produces a practically important reduction in between-coupon or between-build variance;
- substantially improves out-of-build prediction;
- reproduces the expected mechanism-specific observations; and
- leaves no rival explanation with comparable support under the planned contrasts.

Multiple contributors may be retained; the study will not force selection of a single cause.

### Practical thresholds
Before data collection, define:

- the smallest practically important strength difference;
- the smallest worthwhile reduction in standard deviation or failure probability;
- acceptable test-system measurement variation;
- the target predictive error;
- the desired confirmation probability or confidence level.

Thresholds should be based on engineering tolerances and intended use, not chosen after observing results.

### Stopping
Stop after:

- measurement qualification is acceptable;
- the planned number of independent builds and randomized factor assignments is complete;
- every leading explanation has received its prespecified diagnostic contrast;
- the independent confirmation set is complete; and
- precision targets for primary effects and major variance components are met.

Allow one blinded sample-size re-estimation using pooled variance only. Do not stop early because an interim factor appears significant. If measurement-system variation is too large relative to the practical threshold, pause the causal study, improve the measurement system, and restart qualification.

Conclude “unresolved within the studied design” if confirmation fails, rival explanations remain observationally equivalent, or residual variability remains too large despite adequate power. Report this rather than selecting the best-fitting explanation alone.

---

## 8. Limitations

- Results may not generalize beyond the studied polymer, machine architecture, geometry, orientation, parameter range, or environmental conditions.
- Unlogged machine behavior and localized thermal or flow fluctuations may remain unmeasured.
- Internal imaging, thermal analysis, and fracture microscopy may be feasible only on subsets, reducing mediation precision.
- Some process factors may be difficult to vary independently; temperature, viscosity, flow, cooling, and bonding are physically coupled.
- Test coupons may not represent complex components with different thermal masses, toolpaths, stress concentrations, or load states.
- Correcting strength by measured area may not fully represent irregular load-bearing cross sections or local voids.
- Destructive testing prevents repeated strength measurements on the same specimen.
- Fracture-mode classification can be subjective despite blinding and reliability checks.
- Restricted operating ranges may miss threshold or nonlinear behavior outside normal production.
- A factor can explain variation statistically without identifying the complete microscopic mechanism.
- Failure to detect an effect may reflect insufficient intervention contrast, inaccurate measurement, or limited replication rather than proof of no effect.
- Even a successful intervention may reduce but not eliminate stochastic defect formation; residual uncertainty should be incorporated into manufacturing acceptance criteria.

